<p>Leslie Thornton=E2=80=99s film&nbsp;<em>Jennifer, Where Are You?</em>&nb=
sp;revolves around a young girl haphazardly applying red lipstick while a m=
an off-screen calls out, =E2=80=9CJennifer, where are you?=E2=80=9D The fil=
m cuts between the girl, upside-down scenes of suburban life, and footage o=
f swimming fish, all accompanied by a musical score that grows increasingly=
 ominous. As the imagery, music, and child=E2=80=99s facial expressions cha=
nge, our perception of the question=E2=80=94one that never gets answered=E2=
=80=94also shifts, displaying media=E2=80=99s powerful ability to alter mea=
ning with colliding sounds and images.&nbsp;</p><p>Thornton emerged from a =
generation of artists and filmmakers concerned with the ways that film, as =
a medium, structures identities and perceptions of women. Influenced by the=
 theory that the unconscious mind operates through signs and symbols, like =
language, Thornton has made the relationship between speech and cinema cent=
ral to her work. =E2=80=9CThe way language works has been a lifelong preocc=
upation,=E2=80=9D Thornton says. =E2=80=9CSpeech was like an object, an ene=
my, a barrier.=E2=80=9D&nbsp;<br class=3D"softbreak"></p>



